before.statement:
Let $K$ be a characteristic-zero field and $F$ a field of characteristic $p>0$, both in the regime of [[def-nondegenerate-root-of-unity-level-one-hecke-regime]] with the same $e$ and with $\xi_K,\xi_F$ primitive $e$-th roots, and fix the integral form $H^\Lambda_\alpha(\mathbb Z)$ of [[def-integral-form-of-the-cyclotomic-hecke-klr-block]]. For $\lambda\in RP_\alpha$ let $\widetilde D(\lambda)$ be the characteristic-zero normalized simple and let $0\ne v_\lambda\in\widetilde D(\lambda)$ be the image of the generating vector of the graded Specht module $S(\lambda)$. Then:

1. $L(\lambda):=H^\Lambda_\alpha(\mathbb Z)\,v_\lambda$ is a lattice in $\widetilde D(\lambda)$, invariant under $H^\Lambda_\alpha(\mathbb Z)$, and its $e(i)$-weight spaces are lattices in the weight spaces of $\widetilde D(\lambda)$;
2. $J(\lambda):=F\otimes_{\mathbb Z}L(\lambda)$ is a well-defined graded $H^\Lambda_\alpha(F)$-module with the same $q$-character as $\widetilde D(\lambda)$, so it is nonzero and finite-dimensional, and its class in $G_0^{\rm gr}(H^\Lambda_\alpha(F))$ is independent of the choice of the lattice;
3. the transported identification of [[prop-qg8-categorification-transports-across-the-hecke-klr-bridge]] (2) over $F$ satisfies $\Psi_{\rm can}^{\rm Hecke}([J(\lambda)])=D_\lambda$, the dual-canonical basis vector; equivalently $[J(\lambda)]=(\Psi_{\rm can}^{\rm Hecke})^{-1}(D_\lambda)$.

Choice is inherited only through the transported global-basis and duality suppliers.

after.statement:
Let $\Theta=\sum_{
u\in Q_+}\Theta_
u\in\widehat C$ be the $D$-coevaluation family of the diagonal root-height completion $\widehat C$ of [[def-root-height-completion-for-weightwise-quasi-r-intertwiners]], built from the normalized Drinfeld pairing $D$ of [[def-drinfeld-hopf-pairing-between-positive-and-negative-quantum-halves]] and its homogeneous dual bases ([[lem-homogeneous-dual-basis-sums-are-basis-independent-and-weightwise-finite]], [[lem-the-drinfeld-hopf-pairing-is-nondegenerate-in-each-root-degree]]). Then $\Theta$ is the unique normalized bar-coproduct intertwiner for the library coproduct $\Delta$: that is,
$$\Delta(u)\Theta=\Theta\,\overline\Delta(u)\qquad	ext{for all }u\in U_q(\mathfrak g),\qquad \overline\Delta(u)=(\psi\otimes\psi)\Delta(\psi(u)),$$
among families with $\Theta_0=1$. Its generator recursions are
$$[1\otimes E_i,\Theta_
u]=\Theta_{
u-\alpha_i}(E_i\otimes K_i)-(E_i\otimes K_i^{-1})\Theta_{
u-\alpha_i},$$
$$[F_i\otimes1,\Theta_
u]=\Theta_{
u-\alpha_i}(K_i^{-1}\otimes F_i)-(K_i\otimes F_i)\Theta_{
u-\alpha_i},$$
where components of negative root degree are $0$ and the toral parts commute because $\Theta_
u$ has toral weight zero. In rank one, $\Theta_{n\alpha_i}=(q_i-q_i^{-1})^n[n]_{q_i^2}!\,E_i^{(n)}\otimes F_i^{(n)}$ with $[n]_{q_i^2}!=\prod_{r=1}^n(1+q_i^2+\cdots+q_i^{2(r-1)})$, so that $\Theta_{\alpha_i}=(q_i-q_i^{-1})E_i\otimes F_i$. The family evaluates precisely under the explicit finite-support hypotheses of the completion. This is not the native unscaled doubled-torus $\Psi\Delta^{\mathrm{op}}$ relation, and no integral algebra coefficient claim is made.
